---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DayOfWeek from '@/components/tools/calc2/DayOfWeek.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="day-of-the-week-calculator">
  <DayOfWeek client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the day of the week calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The day of the week calculator counts the days from a date with a known weekday. It also shows the
      doomsday rule, a method you can do in your head: in any year, a set of easy dates all fall on the same
      weekday, the year's doomsday.
    </p>
    <pre><code>century anchor  1800s Friday, 1900s Wednesday, 2000s Tuesday, 2100s Sunday
y = last two digits of the year
doomsday = anchor + ⌊y ÷ 12⌋ + (y mod 12) + ⌊(y mod 12) ÷ 4⌋   (mod 7)
doomsday dates: 4/4, 6/6, 8/8, 10/10, 12/12, 9/5, 5/9, 11/7, 7/11, 14 March, the last day of February, 3 January (4 in a leap year)</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example: 20 July 1969</h2>
    <p>
      The 1900s anchor is Wednesday. y = 69: ⌊69 ÷ 12⌋ = 5, 69 mod 12 = 9, ⌊9 ÷ 4⌋ = 2. Wednesday + 5 + 9 + 2 =
      Wednesday + 16 days = Friday, so the doomsday of 1969 is Friday. 11 July is a doomsday, and 20 July is
      9 days later: Friday + 9 = Sunday. The first moon landing was on a Sunday.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator uses the Gregorian calendar for every date, also before it was introduced in 1582
      (and in 1752 in Britain and its colonies). Historical documents from before the switch use the Julian
      calendar, which was 10 to 13 days behind.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
